{"availability":"available","full":"joshuaswarren/omarchy-mlx","name":"omarchy-mlx","description":"MLX-compatible Vulkan and ANE backends for Apple Silicon running Linux.","category":"Other","language":"Python","stars":87,"forks":10,"created":"2026-08-31T22:48:50Z","updated":"2026-10-11T06:33:06Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":"https://joshuaswarren.github.io/omarchy-mlx/","topics":[],"demo":"https://joshuaswarren.github.io/omarchy-mlx/","color":"#193546","ink":"#a6d6e8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-11T07:26:09.124Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-11T07:26:09.124Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-11T07:26:09.124Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-09T18:00:35.541Z","stars":86,"forks":10},{"at":"2026-10-11T07:26:09.124Z","stars":87,"forks":10}],"technologies":["Python"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["MLX on the Apple GPU under Linux, and Core ML models on the Apple Neural Engine.","MLX is Apple's array framework for machine learning on Apple silicon. Upstream it speaks Metal, so it runs on macOS only. omarchy-mlx runs the same import mlx.core as mx code on an Apple silicon Mac running Omarchy Linux. The GPU driver is Mesa's Honeykrisp Vulkan stack. Tensors stay on the GPU. There is no Metal and no CPU fallback.","It is for people who run Linux on an M1 or M2 Mac. You get local chat, serving, and speech to text, with no macOS and no cloud account."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/joshuaswarren/omarchy-mlx/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"a7e503c13a9d98f83c4f8928359d78d762b2b2d1380cc994afc5c47bf21bd33c","extractedAt":"2026-10-11T07:26:09.191Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-11T07:26:09.191Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"MLX on the Apple GPU under Linux, and Core ML models on the Apple Neural Engine.","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/joshuaswarren/omarchy-mlx/blob/main/README.md"},"audience":[],"requirements":[],"fingerprint":"a7e503c13a9d98f83c4f8928359d78d762b2b2d1380cc994afc5c47bf21bd33c","sourceUrl":"https://gith